1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,816
My dead wife's laugh cost forty dollars. I paid in cash, right into the slot.

2
00:00:07,116 --> 00:00:13,764
The machine hummed like a dying heart, spitting out a tiny glass vial of liquid silver.

3
00:00:14,064 --> 00:00:20,328
I swallowed it raw. The taste was copper and rain, flooding my empty chest with warmth.

4
00:00:20,628 --> 00:00:27,732
For three seconds, she was right beside me. Then the warmth vanished, leaving me colder than before.

5
00:00:28,032 --> 00:00:35,208
I needed more. I reached into my pocket and pulled out my very last memory of our wedding.

6
00:00:35,508 --> 00:00:42,108
I fed our vows into the slot. The machine chewed it up, digesting the best day of my life.

7
00:00:42,408 --> 00:00:48,000
It didn't give me money. It printed a receipt with a single barcode and a chilling message.

8
00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:53,628
I spun around. The dark alley was gone. I was sitting at a sticky diner table.

9
00:00:53,928 --> 00:01:00,720
Across from me, a younger version of myself smiled, dropping a coin into the exact same slot.

10
00:01:01,020 --> 00:01:08,028
I am the machine. And I am buying my grief forever. If the past can be bought, why does it still own you?
